// `avg` over a STRUCT-typed column: refused, but by the HOST COMPILER inside a
// generated blob, not by the front end.
//
// This pins a HOLE, not a virtue. `check_agg_operand` refuses a non-numeric
// aggregate argument and names the clause, the aggregate and the column type —
// see wql_agg_avg_str_arg_fail. It never runs for this shape. `el_ty_of_name`
// launders every unknown type NAME to `EL_TY_INT`, which is the same laundering
// removed one level down in the same commit, so a struct-typed column reaches the
// rule table spelled INT, `agg_cast` comes out true, and the check is skipped
// exactly where it is needed.
//
// The program is still refused and no object is written, so nothing unsound
// ships — the cost is the diagnostic. A user meeting this is told to look at
// `<metaprog-blob-subst>` and at a function name the query never mentions.
//
// ⚠ THIS EXPECTATION IS THE DEFECT, NOT THE RULE. When `el_ty_of_name` stops
// laundering — or when `check_agg_operand` learns to refuse an argument whose
// type is outside the EL lattice before the rule table is consulted — this file
// must be rewritten to expect the front-end message, and that rewrite is the
// point. It is here so the hole cannot be closed by accident and go unnoticed,
// and so it cannot widen unnoticed either.
package test;

use logos.std.wql.wql;
use logos.mem.collections.vec;

struct Inner { pub a: i64 }
struct Rec { pub k: i64, pub v: Inner }

pub deem means(src: &[Rec]) { from src s
      group by s.k
      aggregate a = avg(s.v)
      select a : f64 }

fn main() -> i32 { return 0i32; }
